WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
There are several
clauses available in SQL

2
00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.010
for sorting and filtering
data in a table.

3
00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.400
One of the most useful of
these is the ORDER BY clause.

4
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.560
With this clause,
you can reorder

5
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.200
the data in a table by
one or more columns.

6
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.030
For example, in a table that

7
00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.130
holds data on students
in a college,

8
00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.350
you could sort the
data by date of birth.

9
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.750
The table would then
present all students in

10
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.390
the order of oldest to youngest.

11
00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.085
By the end of this video,

12
00:00:26.085 --> 00:00:28.080
you'll be able to
demonstrate the purpose of

13
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.690
the ORDER BY clause
for sorting data,

14
00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.940
explain the different
forms in which

15
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.880
the ORDER BY clause can
be used to sort data,

16
00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.710
and describe how
the ascending and

17
00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.990
descending keywords behave
when used in sort columns.

18
00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:43.130
Let's begin with
an exploration of

19
00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.275
the purpose of the order clause.

20
00:00:45.275 --> 00:00:47.330
The ORDER BY clause is

21
00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:48.560
an optional clause that can be

22
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.275
added to a select statement,

23
00:00:50.275 --> 00:00:52.670
its purpose is to help sort data

24
00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.115
in either ascending
or descending order.

25
00:00:55.115 --> 00:00:58.100
For example, you can sort
list of student names in

26
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.955
an alphabetical order from
A to Z or vice versa.

27
00:01:01.955 --> 00:01:03.830
To get a better
understanding of how

28
00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:05.465
the ORDER BY clause works,

29
00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:07.615
let's explore the syntax.

30
00:01:07.615 --> 00:01:09.555
In its most basic form,

31
00:01:09.555 --> 00:01:12.330
the syntax of ORDER BY
clause is as follows.

32
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.765
It begins with a
select statement,

33
00:01:14.765 --> 00:01:16.790
then a list of the
columns to be sorted

34
00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:18.940
with each one
separated by comma,

35
00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.200
next is a from keyword,

36
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.970
followed by the name
of the table to be

37
00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.905
sorted, finally,

38
00:01:24.905 --> 00:01:26.690
the ORDER BY clause is added,

39
00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:29.380
followed by the name of
the column to be sorted.

40
00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.445
At the end of the column name,

41
00:01:31.445 --> 00:01:34.220
state how you want the
data to be sorted.

42
00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:38.000
You can do this by
specifying ASC for

43
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.490
ascending order or
DESC for descending.

44
00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.330
But the ORDER BY clause doesn't

45
00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.265
limit you to just
the one column.

46
00:01:46.265 --> 00:01:48.020
You could also use
this syntax to

47
00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.210
order the data from
multiple columns.

48
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.535
The syntax for sorting
multiple columns

49
00:01:52.535 --> 00:01:55.085
is very similar to that
used for a single column.

50
00:01:55.085 --> 00:01:56.750
The key difference from multiple

51
00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.370
columns is that you must type

52
00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.720
the name of each column
after the ORDER BY clause.

53
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.425
Just make sure that you separate

54
00:02:03.425 --> 00:02:05.015
each column with a comma,

55
00:02:05.015 --> 00:02:07.070
and specify whether
you want to sort

56
00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:09.710
the columns in ascending
or descending order.

57
00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:12.335
It's also possible to
specify all columns

58
00:02:12.335 --> 00:02:15.140
after the select keyword
by using an asterisk.

59
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:16.910
This is a much
easier method than

60
00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:18.995
listing all columns one by one.

61
00:02:18.995 --> 00:02:21.560
Finally, it's also
important to note that

62
00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.620
the type of data in your table
affects how it is ordered.

63
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:27.080
If the column has a
numeric datatype,

64
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.490
the records will be sorted in

65
00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.065
ascending or descending
numerical order,

66
00:02:31.065 --> 00:02:34.295
and if a column has a
text-based or string data type,

67
00:02:34.295 --> 00:02:35.915
then it will be
sorted in ascending

68
00:02:35.915 --> 00:02:37.940
or descending
alphabetical order.

69
00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.880
Next, let's explore
some examples

70
00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.715
of the ORDER BY clause
in SQL statement.

71
00:02:43.715 --> 00:02:45.590
Let's begin with an example of

72
00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.705
ordering data by single column.

73
00:02:47.705 --> 00:02:49.820
For this example, I'll use

74
00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.660
a table that lists details
of students in college.

75
00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.280
I need to sort or
order this data in

76
00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.430
ascending order of each student's
country of nationality.

77
00:02:58.430 --> 00:02:59.870
So in this instance,

78
00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:01.295
the ORDER by column

79
00:03:01.295 --> 00:03:03.850
must be the nationality
of each student.

80
00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:05.900
Each student's nationality is

81
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.015
listed in the table's
fifth column.

82
00:03:08.015 --> 00:03:10.820
I begin by writing
the select statement,

83
00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:12.500
followed by the
names of the columns

84
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.125
that I want and the result,

85
00:03:14.125 --> 00:03:16.755
ID, first_name,

86
00:03:16.755 --> 00:03:19.530
last_name, and nationality.

87
00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.740
Then I write the from keyword,

88
00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.210
followed by the
name of the table,

89
00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:24.605
which is student table.

90
00:03:24.605 --> 00:03:27.595
Next, I typed the
ORDER BY clause.

91
00:03:27.595 --> 00:03:30.300
Then I specify the name
of the code-name by which

92
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.290
want my data to be sorted,
which is nationality.

93
00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.689
Finally, I type ASC

94
00:03:35.689 --> 00:03:38.225
so that the data is sorted
in ascending order.

95
00:03:38.225 --> 00:03:40.295
I then execute the statement.

96
00:03:40.295 --> 00:03:42.645
All students in a table
have now been sorted

97
00:03:42.645 --> 00:03:45.110
according to nationality
in ascending order.

98
00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:47.060
Note that even if I was to omit

99
00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.055
ASC from the end of my code,

100
00:03:49.055 --> 00:03:50.875
I'd still get the same result.

101
00:03:50.875 --> 00:03:53.225
This is because the
ORDER BY clause

102
00:03:53.225 --> 00:03:56.345
sorts all data in ascending
order by default.

103
00:03:56.345 --> 00:03:58.190
Let's run the same statement,

104
00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:02.950
but this time using DESC or
descending instead of ASC.

105
00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.030
All students in the
table have now been

106
00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.280
sorted by nationality
for a second time.

107
00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.125
But in this instance,

108
00:04:09.125 --> 00:04:10.895
they've been sorted
by descending

109
00:04:10.895 --> 00:04:13.165
or reverse alphabetical order.

110
00:04:13.165 --> 00:04:15.500
Finally, let's
explore an example of

111
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.720
sorting data by
multiple columns.

112
00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:19.580
In this example, I'll sort

113
00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:22.240
each student by nationality
and date of birth.

114
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.740
First, I write the
select statement,

115
00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.660
then I write the names
of the columns I

116
00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.090
wanted my result, ID,

117
00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:31.565
first_name, last_name,

118
00:04:31.565 --> 00:04:34.420
date of birth, and nationality.

119
00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.830
I then write the
from keyword and

120
00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:39.125
student table, the
name of the table.

121
00:04:39.125 --> 00:04:42.410
Next, I type the
ORDER BY clause and

122
00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.000
specify the names of the columns

123
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.800
by which I want my
data to be sorted,

124
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.355
which are nationality
and date of birth.

125
00:04:48.355 --> 00:04:50.550
I then add ASC after

126
00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.970
nationality so that
the data is sorted in

127
00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:57.140
ascending order of
nationality and I add DESC

128
00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.270
after date of birth
so that the data

129
00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.840
from this column will be
sorted in descending order.

130
00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.120
Then I run the statement.
This returns my table with

131
00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.220
the data for the
specified columns

132
00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:08.720
organized as instructed,

133
00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.070
which is alphabetically for

134
00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:12.920
nationality and
youngest to oldest.

135
00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:16.550
This was a short introduction
to the SQL ORDER BY clause.

136
00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:18.500
You can now demonstrate
the purpose of

137
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.585
the ORDER BY clause
for sorting data,

138
00:05:20.585 --> 00:05:23.000
and you can also explain the
different forms in which

139
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.660
the ORDER BY clause can be
used to sort data. Great work.